Agent-requested rule. Ask for it by name when a task touches payroll correctness.

## Kennitala (Icelandic national ID)

Ten digits, shown `DDMMYY-NNNC`.

- Digits 1–6: date of birth, `DDMMYY`.
- Digits 7–8: sequence number.
- Digit 9: **mod-11 check digit**.
- Digit 10: century marker (`9` for 1900s, `0` for 2000s).

Check digit algorithm: multiply digits 1–8 by the weights `3, 2, 7, 6, 5, 4, 3, 2`, sum
them, take `sum mod 11`. If the remainder is 0 the check digit is 0; otherwise it is
`11 - remainder`. A remainder of 1 means the number is invalid (it would need a check
digit of 10).

Length-only validation accepts roughly ten times more invalid numbers than it rejects.
A correct implementation lives in `fixtures/kennitala.ts`.

Companies also have kennitalas, distinguished by a first digit of 4–7 rather than a real
day of month — a validator that assumes a person will reject every company.

## ISK

- No minor unit in practice. Amounts shown to a user are whole kronur.
- Formatting is `is-IS`: `.` groups thousands, `,` is the decimal separator.
  `267521.3152` formats as `267.521,315` — which is both wrong for ISK and easy to miss
  in a screenshot.
- **Round once, at the display boundary.** Totals must equal the sum of the displayed
  rows. Summing unrounded values and rounding the total produces off-by-a-few-kronur
  differences that reconciliation will find later, expensively.

## Tax and pension

- Pension is withheld from gross before income tax.
- Income tax is **marginal**: a higher rate applies only to the portion of income above
  the threshold, never to the whole amount. Applying the higher rate to the entire base
  once the threshold is crossed creates a cliff where earning more takes home less.
- A personal allowance is a credit subtracted from computed tax, floored at zero — a
  negative tax must never become extra pay.
- The constants in `main.js` are a simplified teaching model, not Icelandic tax law.
  Read them from the source rather than assuming a rate.

## Locale — the dangerous part is the punctuation, not the alphabet

**Numbers.** `is-IS` groups thousands with `.` and marks decimals with `,` — the exact
opposite of `en-US`. So `1.500` means one thousand five hundred, and `1,5` means one and a
half. A system that reads an `en-US` `1.500` as Icelandic pays somebody 1500 when they
meant 1.5, or the reverse. This is why money is asserted as a **whole formatted string**
and never parsed back to a float: parsing is where the two conventions collide silently.

**Dates.** `is-IS` is `d.M.yyyy`; `en-US` is `M/d/yyyy`. The same day renders as `4.9.2026`
or `9/4/2026`, and for any day of the month up to 12 **the two are indistinguishable
without knowing the locale**. On a payslip, "when was I paid" should not have two answers.
Any date shown to a user, or parsed from a user, needs an explicit locale.

**Names.** Keep test names plain and international — the product UI is English. What a name
field still needs tested: hyphenated, multi-part, very short, very long, leading and
trailing whitespace, and markup-shaped strings.

## What this means for test design

Boundary values worth having in every payroll suite: exactly at a band threshold, one
krona either side of it, zero, negative, a very large salary, and an amount whose net
lands on a fractional krona.
